course: space
lesson: 24
title: Galaxies and the Big Bang
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Just a hundred years ago many astronomers thought our Milky Way was the whole universe, until Edwin Hubble showed that faint "nebulae" were other galaxies far away.
explain: Now we know there are hundreds of billions of galaxies, and they are all moving apart: the farther away a galaxy is, the faster it goes.
explain: Think of a raisin cake rising in the oven: as the dough swells, every raisin moves away from every other, and far raisins move apart fastest.
explain: Run the film backwards and everything was once squeezed together, extremely hot and dense, about 13.8 billion years ago: that is the Big Bang theory.
explain: The Big Bang was not a bomb going off in one spot, but all of space stretching at once, everywhere, and still stretching today.
fact: Until the 1920s many astronomers thought the Milky Way was the whole universe.
fact: In 1923-1924 Edwin Hubble showed that the Andromeda "nebula" was a separate galaxy far outside ours.
fact: There are hundreds of billions of galaxies in the part of the universe we can see, and some estimates go up to about two trillion.
fact: Galaxies come in main shapes: spirals like ours, round or oval ellipticals, and shapeless irregulars.
fact: Galaxies are moving apart from each other, and the farther away a galaxy is, the faster it moves away: twice as far, about twice as fast.
fact: About 13.8 billion years ago the universe was extremely hot and dense, and it has been expanding and cooling ever since; this is the Big Bang theory.
fact: The Big Bang was not an explosion at one point into empty space, but the stretching of all of space at once, everywhere.
fact: The universe is filled with faint microwave radiation left from the Big Bang, discovered by accident in 1965 by Arno Penzias and Robert Wilson, who at first heard it as a puzzling hiss in their radio aerial.
fact: In the first few minutes after the Big Bang mostly hydrogen and helium formed, and heavier elements came much later from stars.
fact: The James Webb Space Telescope has seen galaxies as they were only about 300 million years after the Big Bang.
fact: In 1995 the Hubble Space Telescope stared for days at a tiny dark patch of sky and found about 3,000 galaxies there.
fact: Galaxies spin so fast that their visible stars and gas cannot hold them together, so astronomers think they contain invisible dark matter, about five times as much as ordinary matter.
fact: Nobody yet knows what dark matter is made of.
exercise: What did many astronomers believe before the 1920s? | The Milky Way was the whole universe | There were trillions of galaxies, all just like ours | The Sun was at the edge of space | The universe was expanding
exercise: What did Edwin Hubble show in 1923-1924? | Andromeda was a separate galaxy far outside ours | Andromeda was a cloud inside our galaxy | The Sun was the centre of the whole universe after all | The Moon was moving away
exercise: About how many galaxies can we see in the universe? | Hundreds of billions, maybe up to two trillion | About a hundred | Exactly one, the Milky Way | About nine thousand
exercise: What are the three main shapes of galaxies? | Spiral, elliptical and irregular | Square, round and flat | Spiral, triangle and ring | Big, medium and small
exercise: Which galaxies move away from us fastest? | The ones farthest away | The ones nearest to us | The biggest ones | The spiral ones only
exercise: About how long ago did the Big Bang happen? | About 13.8 billion years ago | About 4.6 billion years ago | About 6,000 years ago | About 13.8 million years ago
exercise: What does the Big Bang theory say? | The universe was hot and dense and has been expanding and cooling | A huge bomb exploded in the middle of empty space and threw out stars | The universe has always been the same size | The Sun exploded and made the planets
exercise: What is left over from the Big Bang all around us? | Faint microwave radiation | A ring of rocks | A cloud of smoke | Loud sound waves
exercise: Who found the leftover radiation of the Big Bang in 1965? | Arno Penzias and Robert Wilson | Edwin Hubble and Galileo | Isaac Newton and Albert Einstein | Neil Armstrong and Buzz Aldrin
exercise: Which elements formed in the first minutes after the Big Bang? | Mostly hydrogen and helium | Mostly iron and gold | Mostly carbon and oxygen | All the elements at once
exercise: Where did heavier elements like carbon and iron come from? | From stars, much later | From the first minutes of the Big Bang | From the Earth's core only | From comets hitting each other
exercise: How early have galaxies been seen by the James Webb Space Telescope? | About 300 million years after the Big Bang | About 13 billion years after the Big Bang | Before the Big Bang, when the universe was still empty | Only last year
exercise: What did Hubble find in a tiny dark patch of sky in 1995? | About 3,000 galaxies | Nothing at all | A single new star | A new planet
exercise: Why do astronomers think galaxies contain dark matter? | They spin too fast for their visible matter to hold them | Galaxies look much darker in photos than we expect them to be | Dark clouds of dust block the light of many of their stars | Black holes are made of it, and every galaxy has one at its heart
exercise: How much dark matter is there compared with ordinary matter? | About five times as much | About half as much | Exactly the same | About a hundred times less
test: Sam says "the Big Bang was a massive explosion in one spot in empty space". What is right? | It was all of space stretching at once, everywhere | He is right, it was one bomb in the middle | It was the Sun exploding and throwing out all the planets | It was two galaxies crashing
test: In a rising raisin cake, which raisins move apart from each other fastest? | The ones farthest apart | The ones closest together | Only the ones on top | They all stay still
test: If the universe is expanding, what was it like long ago? | Smaller, hotter and denser | Bigger and colder | Exactly as it is now | Empty of all space
test: Which is true? | Your body contains elements made in stars | All the elements were made in the Big Bang, all at once | Stars make only hydrogen and nothing heavier than that | The Sun made all the gold that we dig up here on Earth
test: Hubble pointed at an "empty" dark patch and found thousands of galaxies. What does this show? | The universe is crowded with galaxies everywhere | That dark patch is the very edge of space, where it all stops | Dark parts of the sky are empty, and the camera made a mistake | Galaxies only exist in that one small patch and nowhere else
test: my mate says dark matter is just space dust lol. is it | No, nobody yet knows what dark matter is | Yes, it is just grey space dust drifting between stars | Yes, it is the black smoke that comes off burning stars | No, it is made only of black holes, which we know well
test: Why does a very distant galaxy show us the young universe? | Its light has travelled for billions of years | Distant galaxies are made of baby stars only | Telescopes can see into the future as well as the past | Old galaxies glow younger colours
test: In a cartoon, someone stands safely outside the universe watching the Big Bang go off. What is wrong? | There was no outside: all of space itself was stretching | The Big Bang was much too quiet and dark for anyone to watch | The Big Bang happened on Earth | Nothing, that is how it was
test: If a galaxy were twice as far away as another, how would its speed away from us compare? | It would move away about twice as fast | It would move away half as fast | It would not move at all | It would come towards us
test: How were Penzias and Wilson surprised by their discovery? | They found the radiation by accident, as a puzzling hiss | They had been searching for it on purpose for over 50 years | Hubble told them where to look | They saw it with their own eyes
test: why don't galaxies fly apart if they spin so fast | Astronomers think dark matter's gravity holds them | They are glued together by the light that shines between stars | They spin slowly really, much slower than it looks in photos | The black hole in the middle pulls every star in towards it
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the Big Bang theory? | The universe was formerly hot and dense and has since expanded and cooled | The universe originated as an explosion at a single point situated in empty space | The universe has remained unchanged in size throughout its existence | The universe was formed by the explosion of the Sun
exam: Ascertain the approximate age of the universe according to the Big Bang theory. | About thirteen point eight billion years | About four point six billion years | About thirteen point eight million years | About six thousand years
exam: Identify the person who demonstrated in 1923-1924 that the Andromeda nebula was a separate galaxy. | Edwin Hubble | Galileo Galilei | Isaac Newton | Arno Penzias
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the motion of distant galaxies. | The more distant a galaxy, the faster it recedes | The nearer a galaxy, the faster it recedes | All galaxies approach the Milky Way | All galaxies are at rest relative to one another
